1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,985
Good afternoon.

2
00:00:00,985 --> 00:00:08,533
This is EcoConnectAI, a satellite-driven coastal ecosystem intelligence and conservation decision-support platform.

3
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:11,683
Its purpose is not simply to show a habitat map.

4
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:21,922
It converts satellite-derived habitat information into patch-level connectivity analysis, criticality assessment, scenario analysis and restoration support.

5
00:00:22,622 --> 00:00:24,362
This is the public overview.

6
00:00:24,362 --> 00:00:33,185
The navigation covers the insight behind the project, how it works, its capabilities, the four study areas, restoration, and an about section.

7
00:00:33,185 --> 00:00:40,020
The visual on the right is an illustration, and the figures on this public page come from our early prototype.

8
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:44,307
Every operational number I show today comes from inside the platform.

9
00:00:45,008 --> 00:00:47,153
Now I click Launch Command Center.

10
00:00:47,153 --> 00:00:50,182
This opens the operational side of the platform.

11
00:00:50,182 --> 00:00:56,807
Before working with it, I will sign in, because actions like creating field tasks are controlled by role.

12
00:00:57,507 --> 00:00:58,520
I click Sign in.

13
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:06,882
The platform has six roles, from state administrator to field officer, and each role sees only the actions it is allowed to perform.

14
00:01:06,882 --> 00:01:11,442
These are demonstration accounts, and clicking a card signs in directly.

15
00:01:11,442 --> 00:01:15,370
I click State Administrator, which has access to every module.

16
00:01:16,070 --> 00:01:17,736
This is the Command Center.

17
00:01:17,736 --> 00:01:20,636
On the left is the navigation for every module.

18
00:01:20,636 --> 00:01:29,273
At the top are the study area selector, currently Kerala, and the analysis period, which is the latest run, from twenty twenty five imagery.

19
00:01:29,273 --> 00:01:30,939
The bell shows open alerts.

20
00:01:30,939 --> 00:01:37,047
In the centre is the map, and on the right the selected patch, landscape metrics and what-if tools.

21
00:01:37,747 --> 00:01:42,145
Notice the label on the map: real data, development model, not final.

22
00:01:42,145 --> 00:01:45,650
Every result in the platform carries a label like this.

23
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:56,996
Below the map, the cards show twenty four detected patches, forty three connectivity links, about two hundred and twenty hectares of mangrove, and four restoration opportunities.

24
00:01:56,996 --> 00:02:01,457
All of these come from the latest analysis run, served by the backend.

25
00:02:02,157 --> 00:02:04,366
The map is the spatial foundation.

26
00:02:04,366 --> 00:02:07,354
Every later analysis links back to a location.

27
00:02:07,354 --> 00:02:08,979
Here is the layers panel.

28
00:02:08,979 --> 00:02:13,072
Green polygons are mangrove patches, split by model confidence.

29
00:02:13,072 --> 00:02:15,735
Orange points are restoration candidates.

30
00:02:15,735 --> 00:02:18,399
Red marks the five most critical patches.

31
00:02:18,399 --> 00:02:20,608
Blue lines are connectivity links.

32
00:02:20,608 --> 00:02:27,885
I will switch off the links, and the candidates, so you can see the patches alone, and then switch them back on.

33
00:02:28,586 --> 00:02:38,512
The platform contains four study areas: Vembanad Kol in Kerala, the Sundarbans in West Bengal, the Gulf of Mannar in Tamil Nadu, and Bhitarkanika in Odisha.

34
00:02:38,512 --> 00:02:44,176
If I switch to the Sundarbans, the whole dashboard reloads from that landscape's own run.

35
00:02:44,176 --> 00:02:48,948
Here the model found fifty four patches over a much larger mangrove system.

36
00:02:48,948 --> 00:02:52,384
Kerala is our primary demonstration, so I switch back.

37
00:02:53,085 --> 00:02:56,971
To see the data behind a landscape, I open New Analysis.

38
00:02:56,971 --> 00:03:00,025
For Kerala there are four downloaded scenes.

39
00:03:00,025 --> 00:03:05,021
Each one shows its date range, pixel size, projection, bands and sensor.

40
00:03:05,021 --> 00:03:11,406
The model uses Sentinel-1 radar: six scenes, combined into a temporal median, at ten metres.

41
00:03:11,406 --> 00:03:18,554
Sentinel-2 optical data is also downloaded, but it is complementary; the current model does not use it.

42
00:03:18,554 --> 00:03:29,519
This panel on the right states what is real: real satellite scenes, and Global Mangrove Watch labels, which are weak reference labels, not field ground truth.

43
00:03:30,219 --> 00:03:35,688
The run panel shows what would execute: this scene, a trained model, and a threshold.

44
00:03:35,688 --> 00:03:43,345
Pressing Run pipeline performs the whole chain on the backend: prediction, patch extraction, the graph and criticality.

45
00:03:43,345 --> 00:03:47,978
I will not re-run it live, because it would replace the current results.

46
00:03:47,978 --> 00:03:50,873
Instead, I will use the latest completed run.

47
00:03:51,574 --> 00:03:55,689
Back on the dashboard, I can look at the satellite data directly.

48
00:03:55,689 --> 00:03:57,969
I select the Sentinel-1 radar layer.

49
00:03:57,969 --> 00:04:02,844
This is the actual input the model sees, rendered from our downloaded raster.

50
00:04:02,844 --> 00:04:08,416
Now Sentinel-2 N D V I, the complementary optical view, where vegetation appears bright.

51
00:04:08,416 --> 00:04:11,011
And back to the normal satellite basemap.

52
00:04:21,237 --> 00:04:22,882
Now the habitat detection.

53
00:04:22,882 --> 00:04:24,591
I open the interactive map.

54
00:04:24,591 --> 00:04:32,184
The bright green here is the model's probability raster: for every ten metre pixel, the probability that it is mangrove.

55
00:04:32,184 --> 00:04:35,411
If I switch it off, you see the imagery underneath.

56
00:04:35,411 --> 00:04:36,740
Switching it back on.

57
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:43,763
The system thresholds this probability to get a habitat mask, and then groups the mask into individual patches.

58
00:04:48,355 --> 00:04:50,111
Which model produced this?

59
00:04:50,111 --> 00:04:51,664
I open Data and Models.

60
00:04:51,664 --> 00:04:57,067
The research foundation is a U-Net with an EfficientNet B7 encoder, called UNB7.

61
00:04:57,067 --> 00:04:59,430
That full model is not trained yet.

62
00:04:59,430 --> 00:05:05,779
What runs today is the same U-Net with a smaller EfficientNet B0 encoder, on Sentinel-1 input.

63
00:05:05,779 --> 00:05:11,789
This model, used for the Kerala run, is weak on Kerala: test I O U zero point zero three.

64
00:05:11,789 --> 00:05:17,327
Our four-area model reaches zero point eight four, but mostly from the Sundarbans.

65
00:05:17,327 --> 00:05:22,932
These are measured against weak labels, and the badge here is only a display label.

66
00:05:22,932 --> 00:05:28,470
The ninety five percent card is the foundation study's published result, not ours.

67
00:05:29,170 --> 00:05:30,188
Now the patches.

68
00:05:30,188 --> 00:05:33,751
Back on the dashboard, I zoom into the northern cluster.

69
00:05:33,751 --> 00:05:39,478
Instead of treating the habitat as one image, the system has extracted individual patches.

70
00:05:39,478 --> 00:05:40,560
Let me click one.

71
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:48,832
The selected patch card shows its area, the connectivity loss if it were removed, its importance rank, and the model's confidence.

72
00:05:48,832 --> 00:05:51,441
Each patch is an analysable spatial unit.

73
00:05:53,748 --> 00:05:55,810
I can also search by patch I D.

74
00:05:55,810 --> 00:06:02,527
P01 is the largest patch, thirty five hectares, with a connectivity loss of about thirty one percent.

75
00:06:02,527 --> 00:06:03,059
Now P17.

76
00:06:03,059 --> 00:06:10,840
It is only three point one three hectares, yet its connectivity loss is twenty seven percent, and it is ranked third.

77
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:17,490
The card explains why: it is a cut vertex, and removing it splits the network into three components.

78
00:06:17,490 --> 00:06:22,279
Patches of very different size can have almost the same structural role.

79
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:28,072
Now we move from where habitat exists, to how patches are connected.

80
00:06:28,072 --> 00:06:29,914
I open the Connectivity tab.

81
00:06:29,914 --> 00:06:32,413
Each circle is a patch, sized by area.

82
00:06:32,413 --> 00:06:39,844
Each line is a link to one of its nearest neighbours within five kilometres, weighted by distance and confidence.

83
00:06:39,844 --> 00:06:47,670
Twenty four nodes, forty three links, and two clusters: a main network of twenty three patches, and one isolated patch.

84
00:06:48,372 --> 00:06:50,647
I click patch P17 in the graph.

85
00:06:50,647 --> 00:06:57,544
Its panel shows four functional links, to P14, P04, P15 and P07, and marks it as a cut vertex.

86
00:06:57,544 --> 00:07:00,185
Now I switch on critical links only.

87
00:07:00,185 --> 00:07:05,101
One bridging corridor remains, the dashed link through P17 and P07.

88
00:07:05,101 --> 00:07:11,632
That corridor is the only connection between the northern and southern groups of patches.

89
00:07:13,807 --> 00:07:17,522
This is criticality analysis, the main differentiator.

90
00:07:17,522 --> 00:07:23,646
The question is not which patch is largest, but which patch matters most to connectivity.

91
00:07:23,646 --> 00:07:31,696
For every patch, the system recomputes landscape connectivity with that patch removed, and ranks patches by the loss.

92
00:07:31,696 --> 00:07:33,072
Here is the ranking.

93
00:07:33,072 --> 00:07:35,480
P01, P07 and P17 are the top three.

94
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:39,058
P01 is the largest patch, but P07 and P17 are small.

95
00:07:39,058 --> 00:07:42,911
They rank high because of their position in the network.

96
00:07:43,611 --> 00:07:44,955
Why is P17 critical?

97
00:07:44,955 --> 00:07:46,299
I open its evidence.

98
00:07:46,299 --> 00:07:53,488
The system identifies it as critical based on the implemented connectivity analysis, and shows the numbers.

99
00:07:53,488 --> 00:08:01,349
Connectivity before removal, connectivity after removal, the difference, and the relative loss, zero point two seven.

100
00:08:01,349 --> 00:08:03,432
By area it is only seventeenth.

101
00:08:03,432 --> 00:08:10,353
Its degree is four, it is a cut vertex, and removing it takes the network from two components to three.

102
00:08:10,353 --> 00:08:14,586
Below are its geometry, confidence and the analysis parameters.

103
00:08:14,586 --> 00:08:19,558
Nothing here is generated at display time; it is read from the stored run.

104
00:08:20,258 --> 00:08:23,255
Now I move from the analysis back to geography.

105
00:08:23,255 --> 00:08:27,080
On the dashboard, I search for P17, and the map flies to it.

106
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:33,647
The red polygon outlined here is P17, one of the top five critical patches, and it is already selected.

107
00:08:33,647 --> 00:08:44,932
An officer does not have to interpret an abstract table; the critical patch is right here, between the northern and southern mangrove groups, with its area, loss and confidence.

108
00:08:45,633 --> 00:08:47,319
Now a management question.

109
00:08:47,319 --> 00:08:50,238
What happens to the landscape if P17 is lost?

110
00:08:50,238 --> 00:08:51,600
I click Remove Patch.

111
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:56,205
The backend rebuilds the graph without P17 and recomputes connectivity.

112
00:08:56,205 --> 00:09:07,881
Here is the result: connectivity falls by twenty seven percent, while only one point four percent of the habitat was removed, and the network breaks from two components into three.

113
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:11,861
For a fuller comparison I open the Scenario Lab.

114
00:09:11,861 --> 00:09:15,346
Scenario A, remove patch, already has P17 selected.

115
00:09:15,346 --> 00:09:16,781
I click Run scenario.

116
00:09:16,781 --> 00:09:30,382
Here is the before state, the baseline, and the simulated after state, side by side: connectivity indices, habitat area, links going from forty three to thirty nine, and components from two to three.

117
00:09:30,382 --> 00:09:36,738
Below is an explanation built only from these numbers, and the affected neighbouring patches.

118
00:09:39,929 --> 00:09:42,290
This is not a prediction of the future.

119
00:09:42,290 --> 00:09:46,528
It is a what-if based on the current model output and our assumptions.

120
00:09:46,528 --> 00:09:51,130
One assumption is the five kilometre connection distance, so let me test it.

121
00:09:51,130 --> 00:09:53,007
I choose scenario E and run it.

122
00:09:53,007 --> 00:10:01,786
At three, five and eight kilometres, the links change from forty one to forty seven, but the ranking correlation stays above zero point nine six.

123
00:10:01,786 --> 00:10:05,237
The critical patches do not depend on that single choice.

124
00:10:08,278 --> 00:10:15,213
After finding where connectivity is vulnerable, the next question is where restoration would help most.

125
00:10:15,213 --> 00:10:17,301
I open the Restoration planner.

126
00:10:17,301 --> 00:10:21,206
Candidates are areas where the model saw marginal habitat.

127
00:10:21,206 --> 00:10:25,785
For each one, the system adds it to the graph and measures the gain.

128
00:10:25,785 --> 00:10:27,805
I click the top candidate, C1.

129
00:10:27,805 --> 00:10:36,962
It is one point six hectares, and it would add three new links, to P01, P06 and P13, raising connectivity by one point two nine percent.

130
00:10:37,661 --> 00:10:44,233
The panel is honest about what it has not assessed: water status, legal status, land ownership and cost.

131
00:10:44,233 --> 00:10:48,214
The current prototype ranks candidates by connectivity benefit.

132
00:10:48,214 --> 00:10:52,890
Cost-aware ranking is used only when validated cost data is uploaded here.

133
00:10:52,890 --> 00:10:57,060
So recommended means no rule was violated in the available layers.

134
00:10:57,060 --> 00:11:01,357
It is a starting point for a field assessment, not a final decision.

135
00:11:02,057 --> 00:11:03,486
Next, change over time.

136
00:11:03,486 --> 00:11:11,874
For Kerala we have Sentinel-1 imagery for twenty twenty and twenty twenty five, both processed by the same model at the same threshold.

137
00:11:11,874 --> 00:11:21,753
I select the twenty twenty five run in the period selector, go to the Scenario Lab, open scenario G, compare periods, choose the twenty twenty run, and run it.

138
00:11:21,753 --> 00:11:30,513
Between twenty twenty and twenty twenty five the model output drops from three hundred and eighty two to three hundred and nineteen hectares.

139
00:11:30,513 --> 00:11:33,247
Nine patches have no match, and ten are new.

140
00:11:33,247 --> 00:11:35,732
This is labelled observed, model output.

141
00:11:43,616 --> 00:11:48,716
The explanation says it clearly: both are model outputs, and no cause is attributed.

142
00:11:48,716 --> 00:11:54,787
This development model is weak on Kerala, so I would not report this as a measured loss of mangrove.

143
00:11:54,787 --> 00:12:02,558
What the module demonstrates is the workflow: when a stronger model is available, the same comparison becomes a monitoring tool.

144
00:12:02,558 --> 00:12:04,500
I switch back to the latest run.

145
00:12:05,201 --> 00:12:09,573
Satellite analysis alone should not be treated as the final truth.

146
00:12:09,573 --> 00:12:13,416
A real workflow needs human review and field verification.

147
00:12:13,416 --> 00:12:18,384
On the interactive map with P17 selected, I click Send P17 to verification.

148
00:12:18,384 --> 00:12:22,160
It is registered as a detection with status A I detected.

149
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:26,599
It cannot be marked confirmed until accepted field evidence exists.

150
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:29,054
Now I open Field Reports.

151
00:12:29,054 --> 00:12:31,369
The verification queue shows P17.

152
00:12:31,369 --> 00:12:42,524
I click New task and create an assignment: verify critical patch P17, with the reason from the analysis, the patch location, and the field officer as assignee.

153
00:12:42,524 --> 00:12:43,927
I click Create task.

154
00:12:43,927 --> 00:12:46,172
The task now appears as pending.

155
00:12:46,172 --> 00:12:49,048
This is the link from analysis to action.

156
00:13:05,353 --> 00:13:10,216
To show the complete loop, here is an earlier task from our workflow testing.

157
00:13:10,216 --> 00:13:17,480
A field officer submitted an observation with location, a senior officer accepted it, and the task became verified.

158
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:22,027
This record is demonstration data from testing, not a real field survey.

159
00:13:22,027 --> 00:13:25,185
No real field validation has been carried out yet.

160
00:13:25,886 --> 00:13:30,491
Reports turn the analysis into something that can be reviewed and shared.

161
00:13:30,491 --> 00:13:33,519
I open Reports and select the Kerala assessment.

162
00:13:33,519 --> 00:13:50,676
It is generated from the run files: an abstract carrying the result label, then the habitat map, the graph and baseline connectivity, the critical patches, the what-if result, the sensitivity test, restoration candidates, and the data provenance with model and timestamps.

163
00:13:53,947 --> 00:13:55,981
I click Generate official report.

164
00:13:55,981 --> 00:14:01,958
This version adds a field verification section and is stored in the database with an audit entry.

165
00:14:01,958 --> 00:14:09,045
The Download PDF button uses the browser's print to PDF; the backend itself produces the report as structured data.

166
00:14:10,625 --> 00:14:15,610
EcoConnectAI is designed as decision support, not autonomous decision making.

167
00:14:15,610 --> 00:14:18,005
Even the assistant follows this rule.

168
00:14:18,005 --> 00:14:19,753
I ask: why is P17 critical?

169
00:14:19,753 --> 00:14:25,450
The answer is assembled from the stored run, with the source files and the result label.

170
00:14:25,450 --> 00:14:29,593
It is not a large language model, and it never invents a number.

171
00:14:32,241 --> 00:14:33,813
Every action is recorded.

172
00:14:33,813 --> 00:14:35,259
I open the audit trail.

173
00:14:35,259 --> 00:14:46,138
At the top are the actions from this demonstration: signing in, registering the detection, creating the field task, and generating the report, each with user, role and time.

174
00:14:46,138 --> 00:14:49,471
The platform provides evidence to support an officer.

175
00:14:49,471 --> 00:14:54,187
The final management decision remains with the responsible human authority.

176
00:14:54,888 --> 00:14:57,572
Behind the screens, the flow is simple.

177
00:14:57,572 --> 00:15:00,945
The Next J S frontend calls a Fast A P I backend.

178
00:15:00,945 --> 00:15:09,686
Workflow data, like users, detections, tasks, reports and this audit log, is stored in a S Q Lite database with fifteen tables.

179
00:15:09,686 --> 00:15:16,775
Rasters, model checkpoints and analysis results stay as files, and the database records where they are.

180
00:15:16,775 --> 00:15:22,557
The model and graph analysis run in Python, and the results come back to the screen.

181
00:15:23,257 --> 00:15:25,061
Back to the Command Center.

182
00:15:25,061 --> 00:15:39,626
So the complete workflow is: satellite observation, habitat detection, patch extraction, connectivity analysis, criticality assessment, what-if simulation, restoration prioritisation, field verification, and reporting.

183
00:15:39,626 --> 00:15:44,971
The key idea is that EcoConnectAI does not stop at showing where habitat exists.

184
00:15:44,971 --> 00:16:00,672
It shows how patches relate to one another, identifies structurally important patches, estimates the consequences of losing them, and supports restoration planning, while being clear about what is real and what is still in development.

185
00:16:00,672 --> 00:16:01,340
Thank you.
